Message-ID: <KAe_GGRXx1Q.Opk-vZBUAgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl2-disc/c/KAe_GGRXx1Q/m/Opk-vZBUAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Karl,

I am working with data from CC mice, my phenotype has 0's for few lines.
Is there a way to run a two part model in rqtl2 ?
- If i understand it correctly, use a binary model, for zero and one for all other values
- Then, use the least squares for the non-zero values?

Thank you,
Aravindh

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Wed Jul 20 21:00:17 2022
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Wed, 20 Jul 2022 21:00:17 +0000
Subject: Re: Is it possible to run a two part model for Scan1?
Message-ID: <KAe_GGRXx1Q.0rSM0Y9iAgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <KAe_GGRXx1Q.Opk-vZBUAgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <KAe_GGRXx1Q.Opk-vZBUAgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl2-disc/c/KAe_GGRXx1Q/m/0rSM0Y9iAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It's not built in, but you can do a scan with a binary trait model, for a new trait defined by z=1 if y>0 and z=0 if y=0, and then a standard scan for a new trait where values with y=0 are replaced by NAs.

karl

